"af_nicole|Z\u01ceoshang h\u01ceo! Good morning! I\u2019m Dr. Wen Jiahui, a children\u2019s doctor at Riverside. Welcome to Shanghai.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"af_nicole|I trained at Boston Harbor for a year. I still miss the clam chowder, and I still talk with your Dr. Reyes every month.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"af_nicole|Our case conference is on the terrace of Riverside\u2019s old clinic, just along the Bund. Our new hospital is the white building across the river, the one with the teal cross on it.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"af_nicole|Your Mandarin is good! For thank you, we say xi\u00e8xie. You\u2019ll hear it a lot today.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"af_nicole|That\u2019s Qingtai, a new town that was built all in one piece, with homes, shops and gardens stacked on wide terraces. The old buildings on this side, along the Bund, have been kept exactly as they were, and people here like it that way.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"elder_man|Welcome, Doctor. I\u2019m Lu Guoqiang. I take care of children\u2019s hearts at Riverside. Please sit down. The tea is l\u00f3ngj\u01d0ng, from Hangzhou.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"elder_man|Dr. Reyes sent us Ellie\u2019s case last night. Let me check that I have it right. She is five years old, and she has had a fever for five days, with red eyes and red, cracked lips.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"elder_man|She has no rash, and her hands and feet look normal. So she has some signs of Kawasaki disease, but not enough of them for us to be sure. Doctors call that incomplete Kawasaki.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"af_nicole|Kawasaki disease is rare in Boston. Here at Riverside we treat about three hundred children with it every year. It is much more common in East Asia, and nobody knows exactly why.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"elder_man|If this is Kawasaki disease, the fever is not the real danger. The danger is to her heart. The illness can swell the coronary arteries, the small blood vessels that feed the heart muscle.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"elder_man|With IVIG, which is a drip of antibodies from donated blood, together with aspirin. If it is given within ten days of the first fever, it protects the heart in almost every child.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"elder_man|Then it is most likely an ordinary virus that will pass on its own, and the drip would mean a long day in hospital for nothing. That is why we try to be sure first.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"af_bella|Boston\u2019s hospital AI is called Halo, and it is 30% sure this is Kawasaki disease. Riverside\u2019s AI is 45% sure. Halo knows Ellie well, but it has only seen a few cases like hers. Riverside\u2019s AI has seen thousands of cases, and it has never met Ellie.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
"am_adam|Boston\u2019s hospital AI is called Halo, and it is 30% sure this is Kawasaki disease. Riverside\u2019s AI is 45% sure. Halo knows Ellie well, but it has only seen a few cases like hers. Riverside\u2019s AI has seen thousands of cases, and it has never met Ellie.":"data:audio/ogg;base64,",
